* proposition: partial slopes of continuously slopy bivariate functions commute
	* if
		* f: R^2 -> R
			* has all second-slopes, all continous
	* then
		* on f
			* slope-1 >>> slope-2 = slope-2 >>> slope-1
* proofs
	* simple intuitive
		* let the point of interest be the origo
		* let h, k be infinitessimally close to zero
		* let ~ be approximate equality
		* then
			* slope1-2 (slope-1 f) (0, 0)
			* ~
			* (slope-1 f (0, k) - slope1-1 f (0, 0)) / k
			* ~
			* ((f (h, k) - f (0, k)) / h - (f (h, 0) - f (0, 0)) / h) / k
			* ~
			* (f (h, k) - f (0, k) - f (h, 0) + f (0, 0)) / (h * k)
			* observe that this is symmetric in {h, k}, hence taking the slopes in the opposite order equals to this same, hence the order of the sloping does not matter
